WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:07.060
THOMPSON: I would like to welcome you to today's
Council on Foreign Relations Malcolm and Carolyn

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.600
Wiener Annual Lecture on Science and Technology.
The title today is "Artificial Intelligence

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.779
and the Rise of the Robots" with Rodney Brooks,
Abhinav Gupta and Andrew McAfee.

00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:22.510
Rodney Brooks is a Panasonic professor of
robotics, Computer Science and Artificial

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:27.039
Intelligence Lab at MIT. But he is known in
my family, I have three little boys, and he

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:30.220
is known as the inventor of the Roomba. And
in fact, this morning, I was having a hard

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.910
time getting my kids dressed. Roomba is a
little electronic vacuum cleaner that my toddler

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.840
surfs on and the older kids love. I told my
kids I have to get to work on time since I

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.680
was interviewing the man who invented the
Roomba and they got dressed like that. Rodney

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.580
Brooks, a very important man.
Abhinav Gupta is an assistant research professor

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.560
at the Robotics Institute at Carnegie Mellon
University. He is doing some of the most interesting

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.570
and cutting-edge research we have on the way
artificial intelligence can work with visual

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.950
extraction technologies.
Andrew McAfee is the principle research scientist

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:06.410
and cofounder of the initiative on digital
economy at the Sloan School at MIT. You have

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:10.970
probably all seen his book, The Second Machine
Age. If you haven't seen it, you've seen reviews

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.500
of it. I work at the New Yorker and we actually
used a draft of a review of his book as an

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.151
edit test. So I have read 100 copies of a
review of your book. That was so bad, we made

00:01:21.151 --> 00:01:28.820
it into an edit test. Sorry about that.
OK. A special thanks to Malcolm and Carolyn

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.360
Wiener for making this lectureship possible.
They are here in the audience. We just had

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.010
a lovely lunch talking with them. We were
very grateful. This is an extremely important

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.710
topic.
And I would also like to welcome the CFR members

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:44.160
around the nation and the world participating
in this meeting through the live stream.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.340
We are going to begin with a video to help
us understand what exactly is going on. So

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:54.600
if someone could please queue up Baxter. This
is one of the many robots that Rodney Brooks

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.010
has brought into the world. So, Baxter.
(BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:51.050
(UNKNOWN): Think adding automation to your
production line limits you to high costs,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:03:08.050
high volume, caged robots? Think automation
requires specialized robots programmed by

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.050
highly-trained engineers? Well, think again.
Baxter breaks out from the productive cages

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.050
that contain many manufacturing robots, allowing
people and robots to work side by side, doing

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.050
what each does best. From machine tending
to line loading, to packing and unpacking,

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.050
Baxter has the flexibility to be deployed
and redeployed on tasks with minimal setup

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.050
or integration costs. Whether there is increased
customer demand or the introduction of a new

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:18.030
product, Baxter helps you create a collaborative
workforce, one with increased productivity

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:31.069
and efficiency.
Think about getting all this for a base price

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.230
of $25,000. That translates into an ROI of
months, not years.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.230
Now think about what Baxter could add to your
plant. Rethink manufacturing. Rethink what's

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.230
next. Rethink robotics.
(END VIDEOTAPE)

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.230
BROOKS: In my defense, I did not choose that
video.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.281
THOMPSON: That is an excellent video. I hope
everybody's excited.

00:03:39.281 --> 00:03:41.830
BROOKS: Well, it's a little selling and I
wouldn't have chosen the selling one.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.680
THOMPSON: It's like a Super Bowl ad. After
watching that, I replaced the entire staff

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.370
of the New Yorker website with Baxters and
it's been going quite well for the last two

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:54.280
weeks. I hope you all visit newyorker.com.
Abhinav, would you like to—we're going to

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.780
begin with the hardest stuff first. So Abhinav,
I want you to give some background on what

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.340
NEIL is so we can understand it, and then
we will jump right into the big topics everybody

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.390
wants to hear about, how the economy is changing,
how the world is changing, how the military

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.910
is changing and how long everyone in this
room will still have their jobs until they're

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.769
replaced with robots.
BROOKS: Forever, forever.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:14.750
THOMPSON: Not everybody if I've read all your
work.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.381
Abhinav, do you want to start with NEIL?
GUPTA: Before I start, I think I should say

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:22.389
that I also have a Baxter in my lab and I'm
a big fan of Baxter. It's changing at least

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:25.410
research completely with the price point which
it has.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.420
THOMPSON: Andy, do you have a Baxter?
MCAFEE: I do not yet.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.420
THOMPSON: Do you have a Roomba?
GUPTA: You should buy one.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:32.740
MCAFEE: Well, Rod and I are neighbors. I'm
just going to go over and see his in Cambridge.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.570
THOMPSON: All right. Abhinav?
GUPTA: OK. So before I give an introduction

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:41.900
to NEIL, let me just first talk about why
we need NEIL. And so if you think, there are

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:47.960
things we humans—like facts we humans just
seem to know about. For example, all of us

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:53.620
here know sheeps are white, cars have wheels,
babies have eyes, and so on. So these kind

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.360
of facts, which I like to call common sense
knowledge, it's not clear how we learned them.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.460
But what is clear to me is that we use common
sense every day and every moment of our lives.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:12.160
For example, if you see a boxy object outside
on the road with a wheel, you will all know

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.140
that this is a car. So you are using your
common sense to reason about objects. You

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:21.470
use common sense to reason about physics.
You fill your dishwasher so that by putting

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.949
on one more dish, one more dish, and you know
your common sense tells you that it's starting

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:29.750
to fall. You use common sense to predict what's
going to happen next. So if you throw a ball

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:33.160
up, you know it's going to come down.
Now this common sense, which is so obvious

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.520
to you and me, it's completely tricky and
totally inaccessible to computers right now.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.050
Why? Just think of the skill. How many such
facts can I write? Billions, trillions. I

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:48.590
mean, I don't even—I cannot even count how
many such facts will exist for us to know

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.970
what is truth in this world, what—how does
our world work.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.880
So at CMU, at Carnegie Mellon, we have been
taking an approach where we are building NEIL.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.370
NEIL is Never Ending Image Learner. It's a
computer program that runs&nbsp;twenty-four hours

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:06.320
a day, seven days a week. It's running right
now as we are going to have this conversation.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.130
And what it is trying to do is something very
simple. All it does is it downloads hundreds

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:16.260
of boring images of our world every day. It
tries to understand those images using the

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.840
advances in AI. And as it understands more
and more images, it's basically experiencing

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.630
the world. And as it is experiencing, it is
learning more and more common sense facts

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.850
about the world. And by learning more common
sense facts, it kind of becomes much more

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.940
smarter and smarter in understanding the world
around us.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.370
So for example, NEIL knows our concepts and
relationships. Like it knows that Columbia

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.510
can have four different meanings and it learns
all this automatically without having a single

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:47.250
human in the loop. So it knows Columbia could
be Columbia University, which is like nearby.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.639
It could be Columbia Sportswear Company, which
is making jackets, shoes and so on. Columbia

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:55.970
could be Columbia River, or it could be Columbia
Pictures.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:07:00.330
And then, NEIL has also been learning relationships.
MCAFEE: It's not aware there's a country?

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:04.560
GUPTA: It has not learned it's a country.
So yeah, there is some—there are some gaps,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.560
I have to say.
BROOKS: It's only three years old.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.650
MCAFEE: Sorry.
BROOKS: It's only three years old.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:09.570
GUPTA: It's actually one year old.
BROOKS: Oh, one year old.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:13.720
GUPTA: So NEIL is just one year old. Country
is probably harder than you would say.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:18.660
So just to point out that the kind of common
sense relationships NEIL has learned, for

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.250
example, trading floors are crowded. It has
learned things like Corolla is a kind of a

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:28.000
car, cars have wheels. It has learned things
like bamboo forests have&nbsp;vertical lines,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.900
and so on.
So it has been running for one year, as Rodney

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:36.710
pointed out. So it's still a baby. I should
ask any other baby how much he or she knows

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:40.770
to compare. It has learned more than four
million objects in the world, at this point

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:46.630
of time, 20,000 relationships. And as it grows
older and older, my hope—it might not happen,

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:50.660
as Andrew points out that there are some holes—my
hope is that it will be able to understand

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.120
better and better about our world, using images
and videos.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.240
THOMPSON: It is a terrifying thought of someone
just surfing the web and learning everything

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.060
about the web. They must know a lot about
llamas today, too. I hope NEIL does.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.590
MCAFEE: They still don't know what color that
damn dress is. No insight on that.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:05.740
THOMPSON: Nobody does. This is the biggest
mystery of the --

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.770
(CROSSTALK)
GUPTA: Computers agree on the color of the

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:09.530
dress.
THOMPSON: Baxter knows.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.660
OK. Rodney, let's talk about where robots
are. You've been a leader in this field for

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.210
at least&nbsp;thirty-five years. You wrote your
first seminal controversial paper trying to

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.250
answer the question that I'm about to ask&nbsp;thirty-five&nbsp;years
ago, I believe. But—what are they good at;

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:28.650
what are they not good at?
BROOKS: Robots are good at very simple tasks.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:34.029
They're not good at complex tasks. They're
certainly not good—here's something that

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:40.279
if a venture capitalist offered me money to
build a company around, I would not take it,

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:44.019
even though I love taking money from venture
capitalists but I'm too honest. I would not

00:08:44.019 --> 00:08:49.850
say, you know, give me $25 million and I'll
work on a robot that can do this.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:55.100
[Removes keys from pocket.]
Way, way too hard. Way to dexterous. Way too

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:59.990
hard. We have no idea how to do that level
of dexterity. In fact, in what you saw in

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:06.589
the video was Baxter with either just two
fingers going like this, without any touch

00:09:06.589 --> 00:09:13.299
sensing, or a suction cup sucking. Dexterity,
we're terrible at. We haven't done at all

00:09:13.299 --> 00:09:15.290
well in the last&nbsp;fifty years.
THOMPSON: Why?

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:21.100
BROOKS: Because hands are very complex and
you need to work on four simultaneous pieces

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.819
of science at once. You need to work on different
kinematics. You need to work on materials

00:09:25.819 --> 00:09:30.760
because our hands are very dependent on materials.
You need to work on sensing and you need to

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.050
work on algorithms all at once and we just
have not succeeded.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:37.110
THOMPSON: But aren't prosthetic hands a great
medical success of the last few years? Or

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.220
is that a --
BROOKS: But there's a person in the control

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.800
loop.
THOMPSON: Right. So it's the control loop

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.100
for the dexterity. It's not the physical dexterity.
BROOKS: You need both in order to succeed.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:50.929
So robots are good at very simple things like
cleaning the floor, like doing a repetitive

00:09:50.929 --> 00:09:55.610
task. Our robots have a little tiny bit of
common sense. Our robots know that if they've

00:09:55.610 --> 00:10:00.170
got something in their hand and they drop
it, it's gone. They shouldn't go and try and

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:04.509
put it down. So they—that's a bit of common
sense that, traditionally, industrial robots

00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:10.439
have not had. But we just put each of our
piece of common sense in one by one, rather

00:10:10.439 --> 00:10:15.850
than automatically.
So I think people get confused by seeing Hollywood

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:21.910
movies and see what the robots can do there,
but we are a long, long way from having robots

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.699
that can do that.
THOMPSON: Are battery technologies a main

00:10:23.699 --> 00:10:26.559
limitation right now as well?
BROOKS: You know, battery—battery technologies

00:10:26.559 --> 00:10:34.069
are certainly a—for mobile robots, they're
certainly an issue. But we don't have the

00:10:34.069 --> 00:10:39.129
technology to get the energy density conversion
that human muscle has, even if we had the

00:10:39.129 --> 00:10:40.129
batteries.
THOMPSON: OK.

00:10:40.129 --> 00:10:43.559
Well, Andy, one of your great&nbsp;themes is how
these different kind of robots are advancing

00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:47.720
and what jobs they will eliminate. So let
me just read off some of the professional

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:52.110
affiliations of the people in this room; JPMorgan
Chase, Centerra Capital, Markle Foundation,

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:56.790
Passur Aerospace, New York Presbyterian Hospital,
The Dana Foundation. Who is most likely to

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:03.299
lose their job first to a robot?
MCAFEE: Probably someone like me, who tries

00:11:03.299 --> 00:11:11.929
to survey a big mass of unstructured information,
draw a hopefully correct conclusion or an

00:11:11.929 --> 00:11:17.100
inference based on that large amount of information,
and then try to communicate that insight to

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:23.790
a broad audience. Digital technologies are
now pretty good at all of those tasks, and

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:28.759
getting better along a Moore’s Law kind
of trajectory, an exponential trajectory,

00:11:28.759 --> 00:11:32.649
as opposed to a linear trajectory.
And the reason that we wrote the book and

00:11:32.649 --> 00:11:38.329
what's fundamentally interesting to me is,
really smart guys, previous generations of—and

00:11:38.329 --> 00:11:43.709
above Rod, have been working on a set of problems
for as long as we've had computers. Call it

00:11:43.709 --> 00:11:50.110
half a century. The progress they made was
deeply underwhelming from any practical point

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.601
of view.
And then just in the past few years, digital

00:11:53.601 --> 00:11:57.670
technologies and all their manifestations,
including robots, have been breaking out of

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.790
the little box where they'd been operating
and starting to demonstrate capabilities that

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:07.860
they never, ever had before that are really
impressive when you look at it. They're economically

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:13.759
significant and we're seeing human-level and,
in many cases, superhuman performance in some

00:12:13.759 --> 00:12:19.350
fundamentally important areas, like unstructured
search, like natural language processing,

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:24.299
like image recognition and image processing
and image classification. A lot of these things

00:12:24.299 --> 00:12:30.100
that technologies were, traditionally lousy
at, they're now really, really good and getting

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:34.760
a lot better all the time. This was pretty
unprecedented and unexpected and I think it's

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:38.859
going to—it's already having pretty serious
economic consequences. I think they're going

00:12:38.859 --> 00:12:42.779
to get bigger.
BROOKS: Can I respond to that a little bit?

00:12:42.779 --> 00:12:46.940
MCAFEE: No.
THOMPSON: They've started fighting already.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:51.029
BROOKS: People always want us to fight, but
we don't really.

00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:56.489
I think, although I agree with the general
themes that Andy talks about, I think it's

00:12:56.489 --> 00:13:03.709
very easy for people who are not deep in the
technology itself to make generalizations,

00:13:03.709 --> 00:13:07.759
which may be a little dangerous. And we've
certainly seen that recently with Elon Musk,

00:13:07.759 --> 00:13:13.499
Bill Gates, Stephen Hawking, all saying AI
is just taking off and it's going to take

00:13:13.499 --> 00:13:19.740
over the world very quickly. And the thing
that they share is none of them work in this

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.410
technological field.
So let me explain why—and they're all smart

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.980
people, but I think they're making a fundamental
error and it gets to NEIL, actually.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:31.050
THOMPSON: After taking down Bill Gates, Elon
Musk and Stephen Hawking, he's going to take

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:33.709
down the Dalai Lama.
Please continue, Rodney.

00:13:33.709 --> 00:13:43.420
BROOKS: So let's go back to an example from
the '90s, when IBM's Deep Blue beat Kasparov,

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:48.670
beat the world chess champion. And Kasparov
got up and said, well, at least it didn't

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:53.799
enjoy beating me. That was his—holding on
to his humanity.

00:13:53.799 --> 00:13:58.120
And now, today, you can get programs that
run on—and that was on a supercomputer and

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.369
now you can get programs that run on laptops.
There's about&nbsp;twelve of them that have a

00:14:01.369 --> 00:14:06.250
better chess rating than any human being has
ever had. So people see that --

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.649
MCAFEE: It's so bad now—let me underscore
what Rod is saying. It's so bad now that they

00:14:09.649 --> 00:14:13.939
asked human grand master a couple years ago
how he would prepare for a match against a

00:14:13.939 --> 00:14:22.309
computer and he said, I'd bring a hammer.
BROOKS: So they can play chess really, really

00:14:22.309 --> 00:14:27.569
well. And I think people generalize that in
the way that if a person can do some task

00:14:27.569 --> 00:14:32.649
really, really well, they can do adjacent
tasks quite well. But none of those chess

00:14:32.649 --> 00:14:37.829
programs can play tic-tac-toe. Imagine a chess
grand master who couldn't play tic-tac-toe.

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:43.269
It doesn't make sense. None of those chess
programs can give advice to an aspiring human

00:14:43.269 --> 00:14:46.649
on how to play better. All they can be is
a sparring partner.

00:14:46.649 --> 00:14:51.379
MCAFEE: That program couldn't play tic-tac-toe
without being substantially redirected, right?

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:57.540
BROOKS: Right. So people, I think, are seeing
some of the image labeling that's going on,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:03.519
for instance. Google came out with image labeling,
which is a great commercial problem for them.

00:15:03.519 --> 00:15:08.910
They want to be able to label images. And
one of the examples was, that Jeff Hinton

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:15.939
shows, one of the chief scientists, is, it's
a picture and it says there's a baby holding

00:15:15.939 --> 00:15:22.679
a teddy bear or doll in there. You look at
it, it's a baby holding a teddy—a doll.

00:15:22.679 --> 00:15:27.889
But then if you ask the program, where is
the baby? All it can say is, well, this pixel

00:15:27.889 --> 00:15:33.479
has 10 percent probability of being a baby,
this pixel has 80 percent. And people have

00:15:33.479 --> 00:15:38.470
done experiments. You have a mashup of, you
know, a grotesque mashup of baby parts and

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:43.339
it says it's a baby. It's a baby. It's got
all the parts. But a person says, no, that's

00:15:43.339 --> 00:15:48.079
a grotesque mashup of baby parts in the image.
THOMPSON: But Abhinav, you've solved this,

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:49.499
right?
GUPTA: No, no, no.

00:15:49.499 --> 00:15:51.839
BROOKS: Well, he's working towards --
GUPTA: So, can I --

00:15:51.839 --> 00:15:56.420
BROOKS: He's working towards it because it's
such a hard problem.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.660
GUPTA: Yeah, OK. Thank you.
So since we are talking about images, I think

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:04.389
I should chime in a little bit and tell you
that—so what Andrew is talking about, that

00:16:04.389 --> 00:16:09.019
we have made big advances, again, they're
very, very specific tasks. Given an image,

00:16:09.019 --> 00:16:14.260
tell me what label can you put on that image?
We have gotten really good at this task. Some

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:18.259
people claim even better than humans. I don't
buy that, but let's assume that even better

00:16:18.259 --> 00:16:22.499
than humans. But that doesn't mean we can
do anything else apart from that exact task.

00:16:22.499 --> 00:16:26.439
And that's what Rodney was talking about.
They have no idea that—what does baby mean?

00:16:26.439 --> 00:16:30.410
What does having a baby mean in those images?
No idea. You, as a human, would know, OK,

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:34.069
if I'm saying there's a baby, it has a lot
of meaning inside. You get a lot of&nbsp;meaning

00:16:34.069 --> 00:16:38.979
out of that thing.
So while we have made significant advances

00:16:38.979 --> 00:16:44.040
in the last two years, I want to boil it down
a little bit and say we still are a long,

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:49.899
long way to go but Elon Musk or Bill Gates,
everyone is talking about, we still have a

00:16:49.899 --> 00:16:54.300
long way to go. But there's hope, and that's
what I think we have to see here. Two years

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:58.660
ago, if you asked me can computers take an
image and solve this problem, I would say

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.470
I have invested seven years of my life but
if you give me a random image, it will not

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.860
work. And now, given a random&nbsp;image, it will
work. So all it --

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:10.490
MCAFEE: This was the guy who was doing this
for a living and if you asked him two years

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:18.610
ago would this happen, he would say, no. This
progress is weirdly fast and is surprising

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:22.539
insiders in the field.
GUPTA: Yeah. I agree. And I'm an insider in

00:17:22.539 --> 00:17:25.889
the field and I'm very surprised, I have to
say. Now, at this point of time, I am like

00:17:25.889 --> 00:17:31.889
living in an awe&nbsp;of myself, in some sense,
that—but it was like Rodney told—Rodney

00:17:31.889 --> 00:17:36.450
told us that in&nbsp;thirty-five years he never
thought he'd do this all. I also thought some

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:41.549
of it, like that, for&nbsp;thirty-five years,
I will not solve—see this kind of classification

00:17:41.549 --> 00:17:46.740
performance. But as I'm saying this, still
a long, long way to go and much harder way

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:52.540
to go. What—where all the kind of gains
have come from is the data. And I think—so

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:57.419
technology, this like deep-learning technology
has been there from '70s and '80s. Don't misunderstand

00:17:57.419 --> 00:18:01.080
that this technology came two years ago and
everything's changed. This has been there

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:06.230
for&nbsp;thirty years. It's just that for the
first time in our technological advances we

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:10.090
have data for this deep-learning technology
to learn.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.169
MCAFEE: Let me jump on this because I think
the three of us are really agreeing, instead

00:18:14.169 --> 00:18:17.990
of disagreeing. I chose my adjective pretty
carefully. I said these advances are going

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:22.090
to be economically significant. I completely
agree with Rod that they're not going to be

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:27.360
existentially significant on any timeframe
that we really need to worry about, for exactly

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:31.279
the reasons that you're bringing up.
One way to think about this, the way I try

00:18:31.279 --> 00:18:36.950
to get my mind around it, is there are, from
what I've been able to take in, there are

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.620
something like between&nbsp;ten and&nbsp;twenty really
fundamental challenges that these guys and

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:44.620
their discipline have been working on. Common
sense is a really great example of that.

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:49.279
As I've looked around, these breakthroughs
that we're seeing seem to be—kind of indicate

00:18:49.279 --> 00:18:56.590
that we're making real progress on one of
those challenges, the challenges of learning

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:01.040
in a pretty unstructured environment. That's
a big deal. There are lots of other fundamental

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:04.910
challenges in the discipline where the progress
has not been as fast, and these are the ones

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:08.340
that you're working on.
GUPTA: So let me add another thing. I mean,

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:12.770
it's economically significant but I see the
positive, unlike him seeing the negative.

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:17.340
I see more jobs created from digital analytics
than jobs being lost here because of digital

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.900
analytics.
MCAFEE: I wish the evidence were lining up

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:22.490
with your optimism.
GUPTA: I feel at least, I mean, so what will

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.870
happen --
MCAFEE: What you feel is not really a great

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:29.250
counter argument to the balance of the evidence.
GUPTA: I agree. I agree. I'm not an expert

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:34.740
in economics; that's what you are. But what
I feel that over time what we have seen is

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.840
the characterization of the jobs will change.
I agree that the characterization—so earlier,

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:44.279
we were like if—suppose Rodney's Baxter
goes into every manufacturing company. Maybe&nbsp;we

00:19:44.279 --> 00:19:48.210
will not have people doing manufacturing in
those companies, but we'll have some other

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:51.250
jobs which would come up. And there are two
great examples which I would like to point

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:55.310
out. For example, this digital analytics is
creating so many jobs in health care right

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:59.620
now. My wife was basically employed because
of this—sorry, I --

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:02.460
MCAFEE: How many advanced degrees does your
wife have?

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:05.740
GUPTA: Well, I am—that's what I'm saying.
The characterization of jobs have changed.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.960
And my wife does have advanced degree. I'm
not saying she doesn't have. But what wrong

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:12.590
in&nbsp;humans having advanced degree? I don't
see—I mean, characterization of jobs will

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:15.990
change, our education might need to change,
but that doesn't mean that are not going to

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:20.330
survive. I mean, we are going to have enough
jobs. Robots are going to be helping us, not

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:21.980
destroying us.
MCAFEE: I agree with exactly half of that.

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:25.169
THOMPSON: Hold on. We've got to pause for
a second here because we are dismantling Andy's

00:20:25.169 --> 00:20:29.029
theory without having presented Andy's theory.
A classic example of panel indirection.

00:20:29.029 --> 00:20:34.000
So Andy, will you please present your theory
about the economic loss, and then we'll go

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:37.700
back to dismantling it.
MCAFEE: And I want to be clear; I'm an optimist

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:43.780
about technological progress. I get sometimes
painted as&nbsp;a doctor doom, which I think is

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:48.900
a little unfair. Tech progress is the only
free lunch that economists believe in. It's

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:52.840
making the overall pie bigger, unambiguously.
THOMPSON: CFR, I just had a delicious pie

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:55.299
--
MCAFEE: The question is about how we're dividing

00:20:55.299 --> 00:21:00.559
up that pie. And the classic bargain for the
era of industrial capitalism for most of us

00:21:00.559 --> 00:21:07.020
is you offer your labor, an employer wants
that and you get a decent and better-over-time

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:11.690
middle class living as a result. There is
plenty of evidence that that bargain is under

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:17.250
some stress these days. Median American family,
even after you adjust for household size and

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:21.049
inflation and everything else we can adjust
for, is not making any more money than they

00:21:21.049 --> 00:21:26.650
were in the 1990s. The recovery is gaining
some jobs. Most of those jobs are not what

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:31.260
we would call great jobs. They're not highly
paid. They don't have benefits. They're kind

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:39.540
of lower to mid-low range, fairly precarious
jobs. And we're still not generating enough

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:44.899
of them. The labor force participation rates
for the American workforce, even of prime-age

00:21:44.899 --> 00:21:49.049
workers, are where they were about&nbsp;thirty
years ago, before women entered the workforce

00:21:49.049 --> 00:21:52.300
in big numbers.
So as I look around, I see plenty of evidence

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:57.720
that something is changing with that deep
bargain and technology and tech progress is

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.720
one of my culprits for it.
THOMPSON: Let me read a tiny bit from an essay

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:04.809
that John Lanchester just wrote in the London
Review of Books that I just read last night.

00:22:04.809 --> 00:22:08.200
I came out in this week's issue. "In 1960,
the most profitable company in the world's

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:12.330
biggest economy was General Motors. In today's
money, General Motors made $7.6 billion that

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:17.759
year. It also employed 600,000 people. Today's
most profitable company, Apple, employs 92,600.

00:22:17.759 --> 00:22:23.429
So where 600,000 workers would once generate
$7.6 billion in profit, now 92,000 workers

00:22:23.429 --> 00:22:28.880
generates $90 billion. An improvement in profitability
per worker of 76.65 times, but the jobs are

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.520
disappearing." Which is echoing your argument.
Let's talk about—not about the United States,

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.919
but let's talk about the global economic impact
of this transformation that's going on.

00:22:35.919 --> 00:22:40.389
Rodney, why don't we start with you? You were,
at lunch, telling us a great story about Roombas

00:22:40.389 --> 00:22:43.120
in Spain, which shows the complexity of what's
going on here.

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:49.929
BROOKS: So the Roomba home cleaning robot
from a previous company of mine, iRobot—biggest—fastest

00:22:49.929 --> 00:22:55.919
growing market in Europe is in Spain. Spain
has terrible unemployment. Why would people

00:22:55.919 --> 00:23:00.779
be buying Roombas to clean their houses? It's
because of the collapse of the construction

00:23:00.779 --> 00:23:05.470
industry. The South American workers went
back to South America and their wives went

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:11.899
with them and their wives had been the maids&nbsp;that
people&nbsp;had employed. And people, even with

00:23:11.899 --> 00:23:17.149
very high unemployment rates, don't want to
be maids in Spain, and so people are buying

00:23:17.149 --> 00:23:22.710
Roombas to clean their houses.
So the—it's a complex intertwining of forces.

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:30.330
I don't think it's a very straightforward
one, which the press often takes your statements

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:35.100
and generalizes them to be very straightforward;
more technology, less jobs. It's a complex

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.269
--
MCAFEE: And historically, that has not been

00:23:37.269 --> 00:23:41.720
true. For 200 years we have had a ton of tech
progress and the average worker has done nothing

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:44.370
but get—but get better off and more employable
over time.

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:47.050
THOMPSON: So why is that different? Why now?
Why is that --

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:50.010
(UNKNOWN): Why this time?
MCAFEE: Because as I said earlier, the technologies

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:53.620
are breaking out of their historical boxes.
They're doing things they could never, ever

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:58.539
do before. And they're eating into the bundle
of skills and abilities that we all have to

00:23:58.539 --> 00:24:00.639
offer. They're eating into it in a way that
would we've not seen.

00:24:00.639 --> 00:24:03.559
GUPTA: Can I add something? I mean, I just
want to say that he was saying 'I don't have

00:24:03.559 --> 00:24:07.020
data", but his data is showing what I was
saying, that with technology the jobs are

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:09.130
increasing; it's not going down. You said
that I didn't have the data.

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:11.529
MCAFEE: Were you tuning out a little earlier
when I talked about some of the statistics?

00:24:11.529 --> 00:24:16.950
GUPTA: No, but I'm just saying that—I mean,
that the kind of jobs might change. And I

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:24.450
agree, I mean, that it's kind of—you know
that these jobs of labor and stuff will definitely—(inaudible)—like

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:29.690
in the Spanish things and so on, I mean—but
there is going to be new jobs.

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:34.659
I think the best example I want to put is
my computer learning. At times, like&nbsp;ten

00:24:34.659 --> 00:24:39.129
years old, my father would never touch a computer.
He hated computer learning and so on. But

00:24:39.129 --> 00:24:42.389
now computer is everywhere in the life. What
happened? The technology of computers came

00:24:42.389 --> 00:24:46.159
in and now everyone starts to learn about
computers and now the jobs are like that.

00:24:46.159 --> 00:24:49.809
I think the jobs are just going to be much
more different than they are today, and that's

00:24:49.809 --> 00:24:52.590
good. I mean, I don't see anything wrong in
that at all.

00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:57.130
BROOKS: I want to say that I've become over—I've
been subjected to Andy's books for the last

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:02.740
few years. I've—I used to say, well, why
now, I don't believe now, but I think it's

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:07.399
a little more complex. I would say, by the
way, that Apple, with their 92,000 employees,

00:25:07.399 --> 00:25:15.830
also has about 1.5 million people under subcontract
in China, which their boss, Terry Gou, is

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:20.580
desperately trying to replace with robots.
THOMPSON: So my statistic is wrong but it

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:24.750
may be right.
BROOKS: Yes. Well, because it's&nbsp;—are much

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:30.429
more complex. GM just built everything.
But you know, one of the—one of the things

00:25:30.429 --> 00:25:34.730
that I've certainly cited before and other
people have cited is that, you know, in the

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:39.720
agricultural revolution, we went from a majority
of the population being in agriculture to

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:45.190
a very small percentage being in agriculture
but it didn't get rid of jobs. But what—and

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:51.710
I was reminded of this as I was walking along
59th Street past the park&nbsp;to get here,&nbsp;what

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:58.610
did happen was the population of horses plummeted.
There were horses on farms doing work that

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:03.279
were no longer needed. The only gainfully
employed horses left are here in Central Park,

00:26:03.279 --> 00:26:09.120
and it's a sad, sad thing. And the question
is whether we're going to be the horses this

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.300
time.
MCAFEE: From an economic point of view, that's

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:16.860
the right question. From a societal point
of view, these are our choices to make. We

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:21.059
get to decide whether we become irrelevant
or not, and I certainly hope that we don't.

00:26:21.059 --> 00:26:26.460
But people who are blithe about job and wage
prospects have one pretty important phenomena

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:32.130
to explain away. It's the fact that in the
majority of countries around the world, high-wage

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:38.039
ones, certainly, but even low-wage countries
as diverse as Mexico, India and China, the

00:26:38.039 --> 00:26:43.350
share of GDP every year, getting paid out
in wages, is going down. It's going down fairly

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:49.379
quickly. We've not seen this before. And we're
not outsourcing to Mars. So the globalization

00:26:49.379 --> 00:26:55.280
is not part of the phenomenon there. But the
classic structure in the bargain is shifting.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:59.029
BROOKS: There's a much more complex thing.
You talked earlier—and we haven't talked

00:26:59.029 --> 00:27:03.360
about this—you talked earlier about the
contract between working for a company, getting

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:10.049
a middle class job. Along with that, the company
took on the risk of stabilizing your employment,

00:27:10.049 --> 00:27:15.350
stabilizing your income and other benefits.
And what we're seeing now is a different sort

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:21.480
of company coming along which doesn't have
employees but has contractors. And it ranges

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:29.320
from Airbnb to Uber, different sorts of models
of interaction, but across—even UPS trucks,

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:35.649
FedEx trucks, the whole way that workers and
companies relate, and it's being greased by

00:27:35.649 --> 00:27:40.149
information technology. But not about AI.
It's just about being able to track things.

00:27:40.149 --> 00:27:42.409
So --
MCAFEE: About really powerful platforms, sure.

00:27:42.409 --> 00:27:45.980
THOMPSON: So let's say I'm Barack Obama and
I have to come up with a national industrial

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:49.789
policy, and I recognize these trends that
you're talking about. I see the economy transforming.

00:27:49.789 --> 00:27:53.379
I recognize that robots are coming. Baxter
will only have more Baxters and better Baxters

00:27:53.379 --> 00:27:59.919
in the future, even if they can't take the
keys out of their pockets. What should I do,

00:27:59.919 --> 00:28:05.549
if I'm Barack Obama and I want to adjust our
economy? Well, I'm not. I'm just a guy, but

00:28:05.549 --> 00:28:09.200
you know, what should America's policy be?
MCAFEE: Are you cold-calling or do you just

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:12.179
want to us to shoot off our mouths if we feel
the urge?

00:28:12.179 --> 00:28:14.779
THOMPSON: I'm asking you to shoot off your
mouths.

00:28:14.779 --> 00:28:18.990
MCAFEE: So in the short term, I find this
a pretty easy question to answer. In the short

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:23.519
term, despite the hard work of guys like this,
the robots and the AI are not about to take

00:28:23.519 --> 00:28:28.470
all of our jobs. They can't do most of our
jobs yet. So the right recipe for now is grow

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:32.820
the economy more quickly. The jobs will come
along with it. I completely agree with Larry

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:36.620
Summers on this point.
So then the question is what's the econ 101

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:37.929
playbook for right now?
THOMPSON: Right.

00:28:37.929 --> 00:28:41.740
MCAFEE: Again, I don't find that that hard
a question to answer. Why is our infrastructure

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:47.529
so lousy? Why are immigration policies, as
far as I can tell, designed by our enemies?

00:28:47.529 --> 00:28:51.530
Why are we still turning out the kinds of
workers we needed&nbsp;fifty years ago? If we

00:28:51.530 --> 00:28:54.920
could get these things right, that would be
the best thing we could do to improve the

00:28:54.920 --> 00:28:58.030
prospects for the American worker, and none
of it is rocket science.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:02.860
BROOKS: I want to bring up something about
robots and the future. I think—often in

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:09.039
my talks, I put up a picture of a latest model
S class Mercedes and I ask the audience what

00:29:09.039 --> 00:29:12.649
it is. Some of them look at it and say it's
a car. Some say it's a Mercedes. And I say,

00:29:12.649 --> 00:29:16.869
no, it's an elder-care robot because what
it does is it's got all these safety systems

00:29:16.869 --> 00:29:23.740
in which is letting people drive safely longer
than they could have before. And that, I think,

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:28.730
is a—and it gives people dignity to control
their lives longer.

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:34.629
If we look at the population inversion in
Europe, Japan, lagging a bit in China but

00:29:34.629 --> 00:29:40.090
coming there, too, and certainly, North America,
I think we're in for a pretty hard time where

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:45.320
the elderly will not have services available
to them. And there's going to be a real pull

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:50.519
on technology to provide those services.
So I would be worried about that because we've

00:29:50.519 --> 00:29:57.059
not taken immigrants to—I use my mother
in Australia as an example. Her life—because

00:29:57.059 --> 00:30:01.419
she's not very well. She quite old. She's
not able to go to the bathroom by herself,

00:30:01.419 --> 00:30:08.289
not able to get into and out of bed by herself.
So her life is—immigrants who've been in

00:30:08.289 --> 00:30:13.840
the country for less than three months, want
a job, they come, they're nice people, they

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:18.470
help her with these very personal things but
they didn't come on a refugee boat to help

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:22.951
an old lady go to the bathroom. They—as
soon as they can, they get a better job. So

00:30:22.951 --> 00:30:28.899
my mother's life is a succession of people
she's never met coming into her life, interacting

00:30:28.899 --> 00:30:34.409
with her in the most intimate ways that she
would never have let my father see her participate

00:30:34.409 --> 00:30:39.289
in during her life. I think wanting, you know,
just the technology to be able to get into

00:30:39.289 --> 00:30:43.610
and out of bed when you choose, rather than
when the orderly comes, is something that's

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:51.539
going to drive technology and drive robotics.
So I think there'll be more, not robots that

00:30:51.539 --> 00:30:55.070
don't have common sense, but more simple robots
in our lives for the next&nbsp;thirty-five years.

00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:59.790
GUPTA: Can I just add—yeah, two more points
and&nbsp;first is, like—I'm echoing immigration,

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:04.989
being an immigrant myself and having a hard
time staying here and every given point of

00:31:04.989 --> 00:31:11.549
time is a pain. I have taught—I mean, every
Indian who is here getting a PhD thinks about

00:31:11.549 --> 00:31:16.450
going back to India because they don't want
to stand outside&nbsp;an embassy for a hundred—like

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:20.899
hours and hours and having the pain.
So—and further, our immigration policies

00:31:20.899 --> 00:31:25.420
are probably not treating technology as good
as money. For example, one example which I

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:31.559
always like is that in the green card, like
the permanent resident process, you have a

00:31:31.559 --> 00:31:36.019
category&nbsp;where if you invest $5 million in
U.S. you can get a permanent resident. Why

00:31:36.019 --> 00:31:39.570
don't we have a kind of category where if
you want to open a start-up—if you have

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:43.110
ideas to open up start-up, you can raise venture
capitals, you can still stay in this country

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:47.769
is you want to? We are not treating technology
at the same level as—and I have example.

00:31:47.769 --> 00:31:52.200
Like, I have a student who wants to open up
a start-up. He has a really good idea but

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:56.200
he cannot open because he's an&nbsp;F1. That kind
of hurts the economy here.

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:59.899
The second point I'm going to sound a little
selfish but I think I still have to say it.

00:31:59.899 --> 00:32:05.379
The research funding is, in U.S., it's going
down. In China, it's doubling every—like

00:32:05.379 --> 00:32:10.510
in the—from 2008 to 2012, the research funding
in China, which is going to fuel all this

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:15.960
technology drive, is doubled. You can see
the research outputs. They are increasing.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:20.570
They are doubling. We are going down. Europe
is even better than U.S. That's what is the

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:24.669
most surprising part to me. I would think
given the European economy, they would be

00:32:24.669 --> 00:32:29.710
worse. But if you think --- talk to researchers,
like me, Europe has much better way of, like,

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:34.590
in the research funding than America has.
And I think that's kind of—also tells you

00:32:34.590 --> 00:32:39.879
that you need to invest more in people like,
not me personally but every—every researcher.

00:32:39.879 --> 00:32:44.139
THOMPSON: You made a pretty good case of investing
in you, too. I mean, and I—if anybody's

00:32:44.139 --> 00:32:46.409
in this room, this guy's doing some very important
work here.

00:32:46.409 --> 00:32:49.929
Rodney, I want to go to something that relates
to this and something that you just said,

00:32:49.929 --> 00:32:53.350
and something that, Abhinav, that you've talked
about in a previous panel of yours that I

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:56.190
watched, and that is the example of the robot
taking care of the older person, whether it's

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:59.130
your mother or not, and that is that robots
are very good at certain things. They're very

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:02.179
good at lifting people. They're very good
at carrying things, but they're not good at

00:33:02.179 --> 00:33:09.039
emotions. They're not good at morals. So how
does our society change as robots take on

00:33:09.039 --> 00:33:13.400
a more profound role? And how does your mother's
relation change if she's being cared for by

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:16.139
a robot who's good at some things but can't
empathize?

00:33:16.139 --> 00:33:21.690
BROOKS: Well, I think we are going to see
people and robots together. You know, I much

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:26.770
prefer going to an ATM, which doesn't empathize
at all with me, and in fact, every time I

00:33:26.770 --> 00:33:30.610
go to the ATM it ask me whether I want to
speak Spanish. It's been doing that for&nbsp;ten

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:35.539
years. Doesn't it know yet I don't want to
speak Spanish? But still, I prefer to go to

00:33:35.539 --> 00:33:40.330
the ATM than to a human teller. We don't have
to have the machines have empathy for them

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:45.460
to be useful. We don't have to have the robots
that are helping the elderly have empathy.

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:51.730
I think there's still a place to have people
in the equation, but the nurses and the caregivers

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:57.369
don't have to be doing all the physical labor.
They can be spending much more time just talking

00:33:57.369 --> 00:33:59.309
to people.
MCAFEE: If you define empathy a little bit

00:33:59.309 --> 00:34:03.549
narrowly as awareness of the emotional state
of another, technologies are actually getting

00:34:03.549 --> 00:34:07.679
really good at that. The—there is a system
now that's better than the average guy at

00:34:07.679 --> 00:34:08.970
recognizing the emotion on somebody else's
face.

00:34:08.970 --> 00:34:11.049
BROOKS: Yeah, but that's a low bar.
MCAFEE: It's a low bar, right? That means

00:34:11.049 --> 00:34:13.429
almost nothing, but it's there.
THOMPSON: They probably dress better than

00:34:13.429 --> 00:34:15.389
the average guy, too.
MCAFEE: That's right.

00:34:15.389 --> 00:34:19.330
THOMPSON: Abhinav, do you agree?
GUPTA: I actually tend to agree a lot more—I

00:34:19.330 --> 00:34:23.919
mean, that robots don't need to have emotional—I
mean, there's a lot of research, again, in

00:34:23.919 --> 00:34:27.510
this field. There are people doing research
in the field called human-robot interaction,

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:32.280
which is just designed to make humans feel
comfortable with their robots around them.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:36.149
And I think that's pretty good. We should
do all this research and it's—I don't disagree

00:34:36.149 --> 00:34:41.450
with that research. But I agree that for getting
applications out there, for getting products

00:34:41.450 --> 00:34:45.639
out there, we probably don't need to have
it immediately. With time, if this happens,

00:34:45.639 --> 00:34:50.490
if the robots develop empathy and so on, it
would be pretty—it will be nice but I think

00:34:50.490 --> 00:34:54.629
we are—we should, like Rodney's example
of ATM. I think it's a pretty good example

00:34:54.629 --> 00:34:59.299
that I also personally sometimes feel—I
would feel freaky if my&nbsp;ATM start talking

00:34:59.299 --> 00:35:03.460
to me nicely and I was like—maybe it's like
the education of me. Like once I have done

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:06.980
enough number of times a day&nbsp;at my ATM I
will start to feel comfortable. But if I think

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:10.540
of it this point of time, I will start to—still
feel freaky. I mean, maybe --

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:12.500
MCAFEE: Your freak-out's going to decrease
super fast.

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.400
GUPTA: That's what I think.
MCAFEE: I remember how freaked out I was the

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:19.430
first time somebody out there on the Internet
took a—put up a picture and tagged me in

00:35:19.430 --> 00:35:23.780
it. It felt like a crazy privacy—and now
it happens all the time.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:27.839
I hear this argument once in a while that
our humanity is going to be compromised when

00:35:27.839 --> 00:35:33.380
the robots gain any kind of emotional capability
or ability to empathize. I fundamentally don't

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:38.750
understand that argument. Our pets have that
ability. They've had it for thousands of years.

00:35:38.750 --> 00:35:42.182
We humans are still plenty human. That argument
just makes no sense to me.

00:35:42.182 --> 00:35:44.730
THOMPSON: And my children are quite attached
to their Roomba. They were feeding their&nbsp;Roomba

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:47.200
little bits of bagel the other day because
they thought the Roomba was hungry because

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:49.830
the floor was too clean.
MCAFEE: And if you think about some of our

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:54.839
most vulnerable populations, you brought up
the elderly, think about people—kids with

00:35:54.839 --> 00:35:59.210
autism who are doing a great deal of learning
and improvement with these technologies that

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:02.600
don't mind the endless repetition that goes
along with that.

00:36:02.600 --> 00:36:06.660
I just read about a really interesting smart
phone app that just by listening to voice

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:12.140
tone, not even the content of a phone call,
can help someone with bipolar disorder understand

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:15.580
when they're in the middle of a swing and
intervene more effectively in that. These

00:36:15.580 --> 00:36:18.380
are humanity-increasing technologies, not
decreasing.

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:20.660
THOMPSON: All right. So we're at the Council
on Foreign Relations. Let's talk a little

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:24.580
bit more about the world balance of power.
And you believe that—you were making an

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:28.870
argument on the phone yesterday, or the day
before yesterday, that Mexico was recently

00:36:28.870 --> 00:36:32.261
gaining on China, wages were shifting to—jobs
were shifting to Mexico that are now going

00:36:32.261 --> 00:36:34.099
to shift back to China because of the trends
we're seeing.

00:36:34.099 --> 00:36:38.180
Give us a very brief synopsis of what's about
to happen to the world power dynamics because

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:41.770
of what we've been talking about.
MCAFEE: You mean on the economics front or

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:44.609
the security front?
THOMPSON: Yeah. Let's do military next. We

00:36:44.609 --> 00:36:48.130
have about five minutes until we go to Q&amp;A,
so let's talk about global economic power

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:51.500
and then let's talk briefly about the military.
MCAFEE: I think the shortest thing I could

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:57.340
say on that is I would rather have our problems
than just about anybody else's. The wage arbitrage,

00:36:57.340 --> 00:37:02.630
as a national growth strategy, I think is
going to become dicier as the technology can

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:06.119
do more and more at lower and lower price
points.

00:37:06.119 --> 00:37:10.790
And as we were talking about earlier, I had
the chance to ask David Petraeus and Condoleezza

00:37:10.790 --> 00:37:15.850
Rice a little while back, not about the arms
race but about the geek race. And they—while

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:19.829
cybersecurity is scary as hell, they were
really optimistic that the United States was

00:37:19.829 --> 00:37:24.109
winning the geek race because we do a good
job of generating them ourselves and because

00:37:24.109 --> 00:37:28.069
some of the geekiest—that's a term of praise,
by the way—some of the—a lot of the geekiest

00:37:28.069 --> 00:37:31.380
people in the world still want to come here
to build their lives, thank heaven.

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:34.350
THOMPSON: Rodney?
BROOKS: Well, I think we are seeing some shifts

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:40.530
in manufacturing. China, when you go in and
you talk to the big manufacturers there, the

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:46.020
biggest problems in mainland China are recruiting
and retention. There isn't an endless supply

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:51.410
of cheap labor anymore in China. And it's
now true that the labor rates in Mexico are

00:37:51.410 --> 00:37:57.369
lower than in China.
That said, I think sort of some catastrophic

00:37:57.369 --> 00:38:05.099
event, the smart phone, laptop, that sort
of manufacturing is going to stay in China

00:38:05.099 --> 00:38:09.860
for the foreseeable future because they have
built a supply chain which you can't just

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:12.530
migrate.
So what's happening in Mexico is all those

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:16.349
boxes you see in your doctor's office, which
are essentially one circuit board with plastic

00:38:16.349 --> 00:38:21.040
wrapped around them, that's cheaper to build
in Mexico. It's doesn't need supply chain.

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:26.310
Sewing goes to Bangladesh because you don't
need a supply chain. But China has this asset,

00:38:26.310 --> 00:38:34.160
which is this lock on the supply chain, the
vast volume high-technology electronics manufacturing.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:39.960
And the Chinese government recognizes that
is—got rid of all import duties on robots

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:45.490
and is even subsidizing Chinese companies
buying robots because there's not enough labor.

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:48.380
THOMPSON: Wow, that's interesting.
Let's talk about the military for a minute.

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:51.849
Rodney, I'm going to take a wild guess that
you've been approached by DARPA roughly 150

00:38:51.849 --> 00:38:54.910
times in your career, and yet, you made vacuum
cleaners.

00:38:54.910 --> 00:38:58.370
BROOKS: Well, actually, that company did two
things. That was only 50 percent of the business.

00:38:58.370 --> 00:39:05.780
The other half of the business was the PackBot
and its brethren. We had 40,000—4,500 of

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:09.069
them in Afghanistan and Iraq.
THOMPSON: What did that robot do?

00:39:09.069 --> 00:39:16.560
BROOKS: Doing IED—so at the start of the
conflicts in Afghanistan and Iraq, U.S. doctrine

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.030
was you put the youngest—they didn't say
but this is what happened. The youngest guy

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:23.630
in the unit got in the bomb suit, went out
with a stick and poked the roadside bomb.

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:30.040
They're no longer allowed to do that. You
send out the robot. All sorts of good reasons

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:39.540
for doing that. So—I could go into it in
much more detail.

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:45.540
So robots are helping the military. We see
the drones, which are&nbsp;tele-opped, so that's

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:52.560
a little different. But what we saw in Iraq
and Afghanistan was the asymmetry of everything.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:56.621
What I called at the time, which maybe I have
to change the title, I called at the time

00:39:56.621 --> 00:40:03.001
Radio Shack technology. We would see in one
area of Iraq some new technology for a new

00:40:03.001 --> 00:40:07.350
sort of roadside bomb. Someone came up with
something and it wasn't expensive technology.

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:10.859
It was stuff you could buy at Radio Shack
and put together. We would then seek—we'd

00:40:10.859 --> 00:40:14.730
have to come up with a countermeasure. You
could then see that migrate across the country

00:40:14.730 --> 00:40:19.930
in a period of about three or four weeks.
So very simple new ideas are all the—the

00:40:19.930 --> 00:40:24.310
asymmetry is always harder to battle against,
or has been up to now.

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:28.280
MCAFEE: In addition to which, the other dynamic
that's going on is that we are democratizing

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:33.359
access to incredibly powerful technologies.
And overall, I'm really optimistic about that

00:40:33.359 --> 00:40:39.589
phenomenon but we can rest assured, the bad
guys are going to use the same tool kit to

00:40:39.589 --> 00:40:43.800
do us harm in these asymmetric ways. And the
tools they have are going to get better and

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:46.700
better as well.
THOMPSON: Abhinav, what do you see about robots

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:49.880
in our military future?
GUPTA: I mean, a lot of research is being

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:54.180
funded by military. There's no doubt about
that. And I think one area where, again, military

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:59.270
is being really helped by artificial intelligence,
robots, I think these guys have told a lot,

00:40:59.270 --> 00:41:05.289
is, again, I think the example of—(inaudible)—the
example is—Palantir Technologies, for example.

00:41:05.289 --> 00:41:11.190
They are—they take all—exploit all this
data which they get and they use all this

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:16.360
data to help in cyberterrorism and terrorism
as well. You can track and you can understand

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:19.960
much, much better. You can predict. I think
this predictive analytics is really, really

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:24.670
getting better and better with this AI and
it's going to help us tackle these challenges

00:41:24.670 --> 00:41:30.070
with this terrorism and other things. Of course,
we are also going to have problems in foreign

00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:33.359
policy because you are able to eavesdrop on
some people and they are not going to feel

00:41:33.359 --> 00:41:35.090
happy about it.
BROOKS: Yeah, and just to follow up a little

00:41:35.090 --> 00:41:43.040
bit on this asymmetry. What the—those big
companies have is they can employ vast, vast

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:50.059
clouds with GPUs. And the small people may
be really good at doing Twitter, as we see

00:41:50.059 --> 00:41:57.100
with ISIL, or ISIS, but they don't have the
access to those vast server clouds that—where

00:41:57.100 --> 00:42:01.920
they can do the big data analytics the same
way that the governments can. So there's a

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:04.710
place where maybe the asymmetry hurts the
little guys.

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:08.190
GUPTA: The only problem is that these GPUs
are getting cheaper and cheaper so quickly

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:11.039
that who knows when this GPU will be like
$100 and even --

00:42:11.039 --> 00:42:15.620
MCAFEE: GPU for the non-geeks in the audience
is the graphics processing unit that's in

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:18.360
your Xbox.
GUPTA: Thank you for pointing that out. It's

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:21.130
such a common term I don't even realize.
THOMPSON: All right. My predictive analytics

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:25.119
suggest that there will be plenty of questions.
So at this time, I would like to invite members

00:42:25.119 --> 00:42:30.550
to join our conversations. Reminder, this
meeting is on the record. Wait for the microphone,

00:42:30.550 --> 00:42:34.800
speak directly into it. Please stand, state
your name and affiliation. Please limit yourself

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:39.030
to one question and keep it as concise as
possible so as many members as possible can

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:44.119
speak.
So in the second row, please? Both of you

00:42:44.119 --> 00:42:51.369
in the second row, first and then second.
QUESTION: Bettye Musham. Is there any crossover

00:42:51.369 --> 00:42:56.800
technology between robots and drones, because
isn't a drone really a flying robot?

00:42:56.800 --> 00:43:01.270
BROOKS: Yes. There's a lot of—there's a
lot of common technology there that both—both

00:43:01.270 --> 00:43:06.630
of them—I mean, the drones being the small
drones, not the $70 million drones, the small

00:43:06.630 --> 00:43:12.750
drones that we're - the FAA is currently arguing
about. Both today's robots and drones have

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:17.440
benefitted greatly from cell phone technology.
It's brought down the cost of sensors, lots

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:22.450
and lots of sensors very cheap, system on
a chip, very powerful computers. They both

00:43:22.450 --> 00:43:26.180
use them so there's a lot of commonality there.
GUPTA: I think what I'm quite certain—I

00:43:26.180 --> 00:43:30.700
mean, you can think of it as the planning
for rewards. I mean, the normal robot, like

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:34.220
Baxter, or a drone will be very similar because
they have to see the same world, they have

00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:37.549
to react to the same world and that's why
I would say there is a very high overlap.

00:43:37.549 --> 00:43:40.619
It's just that the capabilities of these two
robots are different and that's it.

00:43:40.619 --> 00:43:44.240
THOMPSON: OK. Again, in the second row?
QUESTION: Robert Kaiser. I'm curious, Mr.

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:49.280
Gupta, if you could give us some examples
of what NEIL can do. I'm having a hard time

00:43:49.280 --> 00:43:52.760
imagining what the payoff from NEIL is in
a practical way.

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:57.970
GUPTA: OK. So I think this is something which
I have to—I was hoping someone would ask

00:43:57.970 --> 00:44:02.960
it. And there was a discussion on the phone
as well that—why understanding? Why do all

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:06.950
this common sense and something like that.
Think—let's think of autonomous cars. All

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:10.619
of think autonomous cars are going to come
very soon. I'm sure we are reading it everywhere

00:44:10.619 --> 00:44:14.760
that autonomous cars are coming tomorrow,
or something like that. Let's think your best

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.380
pattern-matching technologies trying to solve
autonomous cars. So you have to understand

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:24.390
that our world has two kinds of distributions;
things which will happen a lot. Fifty percent

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:29.690
of these things will happen a lot. For example,
cars driving on roads, cars taking turns.

00:44:29.690 --> 00:44:33.079
You will see them every day. There's lot of
data that—(inaudible)—of these common

00:44:33.079 --> 00:44:37.539
things that happen in our life. And autonomous
cars, they'll be really good at understanding

00:44:37.539 --> 00:44:39.880
these common things that happen every day
in the life.

00:44:39.880 --> 00:44:44.480
Now think of rare things that happen. Now
you will be surprised that 50 percent of the

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:48.840
things that are in our world are very, very
rare. By rare, I mean they might happen once

00:44:48.840 --> 00:44:53.250
in a month or even much, much less. How would
you adapt to those? There's not enough data.

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:57.470
Your—all your pattern technology—matching
technologies will start to fail. So what will

00:44:57.470 --> 00:45:00.550
you do in that case? In that case, you will
need common sense.

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:04.980
And a very simple example of this I can tell
you is let's, again, go back to autonomous

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:09.339
cars and think of this as—let us suppose
your car is driving on a road, it's an autonomous

00:45:09.339 --> 00:45:15.050
car. There is a wire which is crossing the
street and this wire breaks on one end. The

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:19.369
wire goes like this. You all know what's going
to happen because it's still attached to one

00:45:19.369 --> 00:45:24.160
point. It's going to show a pendulum motion
and it's going to come back like this. What

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:27.910
an autonomous car sees, it goes like this.
There's nothing in front of me now, I can

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:31.000
go. And it's a live wire so you don't want
to touch it or cross it.

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:34.609
But it has no idea how the world works. It
has never seen wires crossing like this because

00:45:34.609 --> 00:45:38.990
how many times have you seen wires like this?
So we humans can reason out and say, OK, I

00:45:38.990 --> 00:45:42.760
should stop, I should not drive through this,
whereas an autonomous car will continue driving

00:45:42.760 --> 00:45:44.859
through it and it might get electrified or
something.

00:45:44.859 --> 00:45:49.970
I am just, again, a very rare, rarest of &nbsp;examples
and you can think of many more examples where

00:45:49.970 --> 00:45:52.839
you would need common sense like this.
BROOKS: Can I just reiterate that? John Leonard

00:45:52.839 --> 00:45:59.350
at MIT, one of the founders of a technology,
SLAM, which is critical for autonomous cars,

00:45:59.350 --> 00:46:05.299
put a dash cam on his car and had his students
count the events which were unusual and which

00:46:05.299 --> 00:46:08.750
were not going to be in the big databases
and, indeed, came up with numbers very similar

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:10.740
to what you say.
THOMPSON: So you're hope is that through your

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:15.059
software you'll be able to ultimately teach
an autonomous car not to do those things?

00:46:15.059 --> 00:46:18.599
GUPTA: I mean, it might be able to reason
out these things. It might be able to learn

00:46:18.599 --> 00:46:22.349
about pendulum motions and it can say, OK,
if I see things like this, it has to come

00:46:22.349 --> 00:46:23.940
back while it's still attached to that thing
on the top.

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:31.480
THOMPSON: OK.
In the fourth row? Yes, right there on the

00:46:31.480 --> 00:46:32.710
left.
QUESTION: Stephen Blank, University of Ottawa.

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:37.970
This is about jobs and strategy. A little
context quickly. I graduated from high school,

00:46:37.970 --> 00:46:43.170
a rural slum high school in 1957. We didn't
have very much academics. People didn't go

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:47.460
to college, but most of my classmates had
good jobs in the sense that they lived—they

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:50.520
consumed as middle class consumers throughout
most of their life.

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:55.619
OK. Question today. How do we provide access
to the jobs, however many are being created?

00:46:55.619 --> 00:47:01.080
Do you educate people up for those jobs or
do you make those jobs more accessible to

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:06.730
people who do not have the educational apparatus—don't
have an increased education apparatus? Which

00:47:06.730 --> 00:47:10.099
do you do, up or down? How do you do it?
MCAFEE: I don't think that's an either/or

00:47:10.099 --> 00:47:16.510
question. I think both are clearly going on.
And like Rod points out, the bargain seems

00:47:16.510 --> 00:47:21.900
to be shifting kind of quickly these days
away from guarantees of something that felt

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:26.130
more like a guarantee of a job and benefits
to a series of more spot engagements, and

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:33.880
Uber is a really great example of that.
Opinions on that shift are really sharply

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:38.930
divided. I personally am a huge fan of companies,
like Uber, not because I like getting from

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:43.880
point A to point B, but Uber is putting labor
back into the economy at a time when we really

00:47:43.880 --> 00:47:49.850
need labor. If we're not happy about some
characteristics of that job or what people—the

00:47:49.850 --> 00:47:54.550
life of an Uber driver, great. Let's intervene
with policy. If we don't like where that person

00:47:54.550 --> 00:47:59.280
is, let's top them up with something like
an earned income tax credit. Let's make educational

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:03.420
options more available so they can upskill
themselves if that's a good path forward.

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:07.119
But for heaven sake, if we've got companies
that are adding labor to the economy, why

00:48:07.119 --> 00:48:09.990
are we demonizing them? It doesn't make sense
to me.

00:48:09.990 --> 00:48:15.670
BROOKS: But to—sort of your question up
or down, without robots that we're putting

00:48:15.670 --> 00:48:19.390
in factories, we're making it so the ordinary
factory worker can learn—teach themselves

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:23.480
how to program it. You know, if I had my smart
phone in my hand, imagine you had to go take

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:27.380
a community college course in order to learn
how to use a smart phone. They wouldn't have

00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:31.150
taken off as they have. What we put in—what
the companies that build them put in there

00:48:31.150 --> 00:48:37.510
was technology that teaches the consumer how
to use them as they first use them and so

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:44.510
it brings the consumer into that world.
I think from looking at industrial equipment,

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:48.940
the way to go is to have the industrial equipment
be smarter and bring the person into the world

00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:53.569
and be able to understand it so you don't
need to go off and be trained in a particular

00:48:53.569 --> 00:48:56.910
technology that's going to change in&nbsp;ten
years anyway, so it's got to be continuous

00:48:56.910 --> 00:48:59.710
on-the-job learning, and technology has to
provide that.

00:48:59.710 --> 00:49:03.740
MCAFEE: It also appears that about, again,
since the turn of the century, you still want

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:08.240
to go to college because the wage premium
on a college education is a pretty good one.

00:49:08.240 --> 00:49:13.529
But even college-educated people are not seeing
their real wages go up. So this idea that

00:49:13.529 --> 00:49:17.940
getting more skills will always put you on
an upward trajectory of earnings, that really

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:22.650
appears not to be true, at least since the
turn of the century. The only folk who it

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:27.160
looks like are seeing their real earnings
go up are the 1 percent, the 1 percent of

00:49:27.160 --> 00:49:29.430
the 1 percent.
QUESTION: We have a—(inaudible)—core of

00:49:29.430 --> 00:49:34.329
people in this country who are not going to
go to college, and that's the reality. Those

00:49:34.329 --> 00:49:37.510
are the people who need some sort of access
to jobs.

00:49:37.510 --> 00:49:40.880
MCAFEE: Which is why I'm a huge fan of Uber.
BROOKS: Yeah, those Uber drivers don't have

00:49:40.880 --> 00:49:44.490
to go to college and they're still being able
to be—do something pretty good.

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:48.870
THOMPSON: OK. Do we have a next question here
on the right? Whichever one of you --

00:49:48.870 --> 00:49:58.640
QUESTION: Steve Rodriguez. I'm a venture partner
with a VC company here in New York. So an

00:49:58.640 --> 00:50:04.539
area I focus on is technology with military
applications. What in your, I guess, estimation,

00:50:04.539 --> 00:50:11.080
is the biggest barriers to companies like
iRobot or even like Helen Greiner's company,

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:15.370
CyPhy, is a former colleague of yours? What's
their biggest barrier to entering or dealing

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:20.130
with the military, from your perspective?
Yeah, I'll stick with one question.

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:26.690
BROOKS: Having living that with iRobot, we
would not have gotten anywhere near as far

00:50:26.690 --> 00:50:33.299
if there hadn't been some wars going on that
we had an answer for. Very hard to break into

00:50:33.299 --> 00:50:40.280
the acquisition cycle for a smaller company.
The bigger companies really do have it locked

00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:44.550
down pretty well and it's very hard to get
in. It was just that we had a technology that

00:50:44.550 --> 00:50:53.089
no one else had but it never got into a program
of record. As soon as the wars ran down, iRobot's

00:50:53.089 --> 00:50:59.380
share of—the percentage of their military
business decreased rapidly within the company.

00:50:59.380 --> 00:51:05.930
So it's the—the companies that are there
are still taking enormous parts of the military

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:11.661
budget on things, you know, as throw weight,
if you like, whereas maybe the threats of

00:51:11.661 --> 00:51:15.599
the future are going to be very different
sorts of threats, and that worries me.

00:51:15.599 --> 00:51:20.580
THOMPSON: The gentleman who is also in the
fourth row?

00:51:20.580 --> 00:51:25.890
QUESTION: Les Baquiran, Alpine Capital Advisors.
My question is that I think we could all agree

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:30.609
that technology has lowered the price for
consumers in a lot of different things, but

00:51:30.609 --> 00:51:35.900
there's two core industries that it really
hasn't impacted, at least to my expectations,

00:51:35.900 --> 00:51:41.049
and that's really health care and education,
because health care and education have far

00:51:41.049 --> 00:51:47.430
outpaced normal inflation. So what do you
see in the future in those particular sectors,

00:51:47.430 --> 00:51:51.760
technology, AI, robotics, helping to lower
the cost of both?

00:51:51.760 --> 00:51:55.520
MCAFEE: Larry Summers makes this point in
a really vivid way. He says if you go look

00:51:55.520 --> 00:51:59.830
at the bundle of stuff that makes up the CPI,
the Consumer Price Index, where we get our

00:51:59.830 --> 00:52:06.210
inflation figures from, it was set to 100
in—I'm going to get the year wrong—like&nbsp;thirty

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:08.280
years ago,&nbsp;forty years ago.
(UNKNOWN): (OFF MIKE)

00:52:08.280 --> 00:52:09.609
MCAFEE: '83?
(UNKNOWN): '82.

00:52:09.609 --> 00:52:15.900
MCAFEE: '82? So that—everything was 100
in '82. A color TV today is 10. A hospital

00:52:15.900 --> 00:52:22.330
visit and a college education are at 600.
So I couldn't agree more. We are not seeing

00:52:22.330 --> 00:52:27.750
the kind of crazy price declines in these
huge industries that we're seeing elsewhere.

00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:33.590
What's going to bring them there? Opening
up these markets, getting rid of the incumbent's

00:52:33.590 --> 00:52:38.680
advantage. Education, my home industry, is
a cartel. And the only thing that I think

00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:43.640
is going to change the real price of education
is when employers start valuing signals that

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:48.559
don't come with an expensive—other than
an expensive degree from an accredited university.

00:52:48.559 --> 00:52:51.529
I can't wait for that day to come.
BROOKS: Let me talk about health care very

00:52:51.529 --> 00:52:56.150
briefly. I think where it impacts health care
is when you change the rules and change the

00:52:56.150 --> 00:53:04.440
way it works. So putting sensors that detect
when a person is going to need help is going

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:09.720
to—so that you intercede earlier in the
situation, don't have them going into hospitals,

00:53:09.720 --> 00:53:13.990
staying there. That's going to start changing
things because the hospital visits continue

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:17.140
to increase.
We're seeing companies start to do that. It's

00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:23.099
getting bundled with things like Nest, with
home security systems. And it's going to be

00:53:23.099 --> 00:53:31.150
that children of the—their parents investing
in that technology to keep their parents healthier

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:37.150
longer at home.
And then there's—most of—a big part of

00:53:37.150 --> 00:53:42.391
health care costs go to the last few months
of life and that's a big moral issue. How

00:53:42.391 --> 00:53:47.089
do we rethink what we want for ourselves,
you know, in the last few months of life?

00:53:47.089 --> 00:53:52.640
And the problem is you never know when the
last few months are so it's a terrible bargain

00:53:52.640 --> 00:53:55.751
you have to make.
GUPTA: I think the internet of things is basically

00:53:55.751 --> 00:54:00.210
what you are pointing out is that the sensors,
the smart homes and everything, I think they're

00:54:00.210 --> 00:54:04.680
really going to change health care, and especially
once we have enough analytics on them. We

00:54:04.680 --> 00:54:09.670
are still in the process of developing data.
I think it's the—I mean, for me, when I

00:54:09.670 --> 00:54:13.730
think, it's all about data. And now, at this
point of time, we don't have enough data in

00:54:13.730 --> 00:54:16.910
the health are, but because we are now starting
to purchase a sensor that's become so, so

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:22.470
cheap, it's now at a point where it's starting
to go and basically create applications out

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:24.280
of this.
BROOKS: Even simple things right now. Some

00:54:24.280 --> 00:54:28.500
companies are putting sensors in—they notice
when a person isn't getting out of bed and

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:34.779
they—the health supplier then sends someone
to check on them before they sit there for

00:54:34.779 --> 00:54:37.359
three days and get into a chronic situation.
THOMPSON: My sensor just went off and said

00:54:37.359 --> 00:54:44.190
there are more questions. Over here, please?
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE), from McKenzie. Mentioning

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:49.510
the internet of things, the actual internet,
we've been reminded vividly, is very hackable,

00:54:49.510 --> 00:54:54.890
very insecure and we're anxious about it.
So as we move the internet of things and more

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:58.589
robots, are you worrying more about making
sure your stuff is hack-free? How are you

00:54:58.589 --> 00:55:03.940
thinking about that, Rodney?
BROOKS: That's a real problem.

00:55:03.940 --> 00:55:14.349
THOMPSON: Have you ever had Baxter get hacked?
BROOKS: Well, interestingly, you know, Baxter,

00:55:14.349 --> 00:55:18.539
we started selling him to small companies
that didn't have networks, so Baxter is currently

00:55:18.539 --> 00:55:25.109
not networked. So we have not had Baxter hacked.
GUPTA: I think it is a real fear. I mean,

00:55:25.109 --> 00:55:30.160
there is the more and more power goes into
the hand of computers. This is&nbsp;going to—but

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:34.770
I think, again, the hope is that we are—I
mean, there is lots of research a lot of work

00:55:34.770 --> 00:55:39.240
on this field and hopefully we are not going
to—I mean, hopefully we are not—will be

00:55:39.240 --> 00:55:46.349
in a bad position. So that has to be a downside.
THOMPSON: Wow. Pure pessimism across the panel.

00:55:46.349 --> 00:55:53.940
OK. Back on the left, please?
QUESTION: Darryll Hendricks. The panel, earlier,

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:58.039
made it clear you didn't think there was an
imminent existential threat from, you know,

00:55:58.039 --> 00:56:03.260
robots. And I'd be interested in the panel's
views on consciousness. Is that something

00:56:03.260 --> 00:56:08.900
that you see as, you know, coming in decades,
coming, you know, hundreds of years, or never?

00:56:08.900 --> 00:56:14.069
THOMPSON: That is a hard question.
BROOKS: Well, let me say I've written about

00:56:14.069 --> 00:56:20.599
this some. In principle, we can have conscious
machines because I believe we are machines.

00:56:20.599 --> 00:56:26.059
You know, when you take biology 101 it doesn't
say "and then the two molecules, you know,

00:56:26.059 --> 00:56:32.020
and the receptors come near to each other
and the soul intervenes and they lock together".

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:36.750
We have a mechanistic description of ourselves
and that's what modern science is based on.

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:41.990
Whether we're smart enough to build such a
machine is one question, and whether we understand

00:56:41.990 --> 00:56:48.859
it enough at the moment for it to be a problem
that we can work on today, I think is a totally

00:56:48.859 --> 00:56:54.849
different question. Some questions people
had back in the days of Leonardo da Vinci

00:56:54.849 --> 00:56:58.880
but it took hundreds of years to get there.
The conscious—in my group, my lab, I used

00:56:58.880 --> 00:57:05.530
to—we used to call it "the C word". We were
not allowed to say it because—and we had

00:57:05.530 --> 00:57:10.140
this deal where we'd go to the international
consciousness conferences once every&nbsp;ten

00:57:10.140 --> 00:57:15.240
years to see if there'd been any progress.
And so far, I haven't detected any.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:18.760
THOMPSON: Can you—let's pause for a second.
I didn't ask you to define deep learning earlier

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:21.280
in the panel, but I was tempted. I'm going
to ask you to try to define consciousness

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:25.079
as it applies to a robot.
BROOKS: Oh, I can't define it as it applies

00:57:25.079 --> 00:57:31.170
to a person. I mean, we only have this --
MCAFEE: I've heard it called a suitcase word,

00:57:31.170 --> 00:57:35.880
in that you put in it whatever you want and
you kind of carry it around. Philosophers

00:57:35.880 --> 00:57:40.130
have been debating what this thing is for
hundreds of years. It's an incredibly hard

00:57:40.130 --> 00:57:43.910
problem. I couldn't agree—I rarely find
myself saying this, I could not agree with

00:57:43.910 --> 00:57:51.130
Rod more on this. Conceptually, we're machines.
Conceptually, there's no reason that we can't—we

00:57:51.130 --> 00:57:54.799
don't have the faintest idea of how the brain
actually works. We understand some things

00:57:54.799 --> 00:57:57.869
pretty well. This is a huge mystery.
THOMPSON: All right. And a&nbsp;new rule; when

00:57:57.869 --> 00:58:00.519
you ask a question, please identify whether
you're a human or a machine.

00:58:00.519 --> 00:58:12.049
In the front row, please?
QUESTION: Hi, Laeticia Garriott de Cayeux

00:58:12.049 --> 00:58:17.290
and I'm a humanoid. So you all took, across
the panel, the position that we're not going

00:58:17.290 --> 00:58:24.319
to be the generation that pull the plugs for
this AI that surpasses us, and that's your

00:58:24.319 --> 00:58:30.579
opinion. Can we hear arguments other than—and
we hear you, it's extremely hard. Computational

00:58:30.579 --> 00:58:37.470
power is not today the capability where it
should be. But what are your arguments that

00:58:37.470 --> 00:58:41.910
we should trust and go with your opinion and
not the opinion of Elon Musk, Stephen Hawking

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:45.839
and others?
BROOKS: So if we look at actually one of the

00:58:45.839 --> 00:58:51.309
places which makes this prediction, did a
careful study of the last&nbsp;sixty years of

00:58:51.309 --> 00:58:57.549
such predictions, the mode of where they—the
predictions were—were always&nbsp;fifteen to&nbsp;twenty

00:58:57.549 --> 00:59:03.260
years in the future. So we know the vast majority
of those predictions were totally wrong because

00:59:03.260 --> 00:59:09.690
everyone always predicts&nbsp;fifteen to&nbsp;twenty
years in the future. So historically, people

00:59:09.690 --> 00:59:15.609
have been wrong in predicting. Why would they
be right now? We have no reason to believe—to

00:59:15.609 --> 00:59:19.632
know that they're right now. And certainly,
Elon Musk has no reason to believe it. He's

00:59:19.632 --> 00:59:23.980
just making that up.
I liken it to—this whole thing about the

00:59:23.980 --> 00:59:28.660
singularity, I liken it&nbsp;to witches. We used
to burn witches. Why did we burn witches,

00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:32.720
because we believed by existed and then we
found them. So if you believe there's going

00:59:32.720 --> 00:59:38.619
to be a singularity where there's, you know,
the great technology changes us and lets us

00:59:38.619 --> 00:59:43.279
live forever and have everything we want,
then you're going to expect it's going to

00:59:43.279 --> 00:59:46.599
happen on a date.
QUESTION: Yeah, I'm asking from our perspective,

00:59:46.599 --> 00:59:50.299
not their perspective. I'm just asking yours.
BROOKS: Because I've been working in this

00:59:50.299 --> 00:59:54.320
for&nbsp;forty years and we've made that much
progress on that much.

00:59:54.320 --> 01:00:00.029
MCAFEE: I went to a conference in January
of this year exactly devoted to this question,

01:00:00.029 --> 01:00:03.950
where there were people who spent their careers
in the discipline who believe very differently

01:00:03.950 --> 01:00:09.609
than you do, who were giving these predictions
about 2035, 2040. What was interesting is,

01:00:09.609 --> 01:00:14.549
again, they were&nbsp;twenty years from right
now, which is the common thing that the singulatarians

01:00:14.549 --> 01:00:21.069
always say. Their point was that we have made
progress on one or two of those really, really

01:00:21.069 --> 01:00:26.450
fundamental challenges and we feel like we
are on a trajectory that looks like this for

01:00:26.450 --> 01:00:30.940
making progress on the rest of them because
we've got so much computational power. We've

01:00:30.940 --> 01:00:34.950
got better insights into the brain. We can
map things down to the individual neuron on

01:00:34.950 --> 01:00:38.950
the brain that's going to let us understand
how it works. I hear their arguments. I'm

01:00:38.950 --> 01:00:41.770
still with you on this.
BROOKS: Elon Musk was at that.

01:00:41.770 --> 01:00:43.040
MCAFEE: Yes, he was.
THOMPSON: Abhinav, where are you on singularity

01:00:43.040 --> 01:00:45.589
and --
GUPTA: Yeah. So I actually think it's very—(inaudible)—and

01:00:45.589 --> 01:00:50.100
there's a very good reason to believe so.
First of all, what does singularity mean?

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:54.450
I mean, what are we talking about here? We
are talking about computers deciding themselves

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:58.829
what is good, what is bad. Right now, we are
making all these applications with a task

01:00:58.829 --> 01:01:03.819
in mind. And we are thinking of making a computer
with no task deciding what is good for themselves,

01:01:03.819 --> 01:01:08.930
what is bad for themselves and just hypothesizing
these kinds of questions which we need to

01:01:08.930 --> 01:01:13.519
solve. And I'm 100 percent sure there's actually
no serious researcher who's thinking of these

01:01:13.519 --> 01:01:17.250
questions at this point of time.
BROOKS: But I—I want to say one more thing.

01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:20.980
MCAFEE: Rod feels strongly about this.
BROOKS: That the basis of this belief is that

01:01:20.980 --> 01:01:25.059
AI is going to get so good that it's going
to self-improve, it's going to rewrite itself.

01:01:25.059 --> 01:01:30.660
We do not have a single system that can write
a ten-line program, like any person who takes

01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:35.789
a first semester of programming. It cannot
restructure it, even in a ten-line program.

01:01:35.789 --> 01:01:39.000
If we were going to have something that could
take these enormously complex systems with

01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:44.359
billions of lines, surely we should've gone
somewhere between zero and&nbsp;ten lines of code.

01:01:44.359 --> 01:01:47.090
We're not there.
So what this whole argument has made me do

01:01:47.090 --> 01:01:50.400
is decide, that's my project. I want to build
that system.

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:54.590
MCAFEE: At the same time—I'll take the other
side of this argument. At the same time, there

01:01:54.590 --> 01:01:59.450
was just a paper published in Nature. A pretty
serious team of geeks out of London built

01:01:59.450 --> 01:02:05.579
a machine to learn to play '80s vintage Atari
videogames without being taught the rules

01:02:05.579 --> 01:02:11.299
or any of the right strategies for playing
them. That computer is now the world's best

01:02:11.299 --> 01:02:15.380
Space Invaders player. It's now the world's
best Breakout player. You will never score

01:02:15.380 --> 01:02:19.069
a point from it in Pong. It's a really impressive
achievement. I'm still with you.

01:02:19.069 --> 01:02:23.990
GUPTA: Again, the task is defined here. It
has to do good on that task. This whole question

01:02:23.990 --> 01:02:27.779
of meta-thinking—and this is what Rodney
is saying—what—I mean, we need to solve

01:02:27.779 --> 01:02:31.700
meta-thinking and I don't think - I'm pretty
serious about it. There's no one actually

01:02:31.700 --> 01:02:35.210
who's taking on meta-thinking right now. We
are still at a much lower level. We still

01:02:35.210 --> 01:02:38.609
want to solve simple tasks.
BROOKS: And last thing. Before we get robot—before

01:02:38.609 --> 01:02:43.369
we get AI that's going to take over the world,
we're going to get&nbsp;really annoying AIs. And

01:02:43.369 --> 01:02:49.200
we'll be annoyed at them and stop them. It's
not a Hollywood movie where suddenly it comes.

01:02:49.200 --> 01:02:53.940
THOMPSON: So the award for the question that
most flummoxed the panel goes to the woman

01:02:53.940 --> 01:03:00.519
in the front row.
Now in the fourth row, please?

01:03:00.519 --> 01:03:01.730
BROOKS: She's a robot.
QUESTION: Rick Thoman, Corporative Perspectives,

01:03:01.730 --> 01:03:06.160
Columbia University and also former executive
at Xerox and IBM. I wanted to talk a little

01:03:06.160 --> 01:03:10.460
bit about the futurologist aspect of all of
this. You know, a major consulting firm was

01:03:10.460 --> 01:03:15.849
asked by a big telecom&nbsp;twenty-five years
ago to do a study of how big the world's cell

01:03:15.849 --> 01:03:21.690
phone business could get. And they concluded
it could get to be 150,000 units. Now of course,

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:26.900
they couldn't image that the cell phones didn't
weigh&nbsp;twelve pounds and didn't cost $7,000.

01:03:26.900 --> 01:03:32.800
So talk to me about a world of robots in which
they costs $100 or $10. And are there such

01:03:32.800 --> 01:03:36.860
a thing—is it imaginable that there could
be personal robots that are two inches by

01:03:36.860 --> 01:03:42.800
two inches or one foot by one foot? So --
MCAFEE: It's not imaginable; it's guaranteed.

01:03:42.800 --> 01:03:48.390
I completely agree; futurology is a cheap
ploy and very few futurologists ever get called

01:03:48.390 --> 01:03:50.960
on how bad their predictions actually are.
QUESTION: Right. Exactly.

01:03:50.960 --> 01:03:56.769
MCAFEE: So it's a sucker's game. The things
that I can say with great confidence, Moore’s

01:03:56.769 --> 01:04:01.390
Law, this exponential improvement in the elements&nbsp;of
computing is not about to run out of gas.

01:04:01.390 --> 01:04:05.950
We've got a generation more of it to go. The
amount of data available in the world is going

01:04:05.950 --> 01:04:11.369
to continue to explode, especially as the
internet of things and all these sensors happen.

01:04:11.369 --> 01:04:16.109
And geeks out there are going to take that
computational power and that ocean of data

01:04:16.109 --> 01:04:19.500
and do things that are going to astonish us.
Those three things I know.

01:04:19.500 --> 01:04:22.579
BROOKS: I agree.
THOMPSON: In the middle, on the—in the right?

01:04:22.579 --> 01:04:27.299
Yes, please.
QUESTION: Hi, David Preiser, I believe I'm

01:04:27.299 --> 01:04:35.940
a human although Gödel would tell us it's
not possible to actually know. So I read your

01:04:35.940 --> 01:04:39.819
book. I thought it was great. The --
MCAFEE: Thank you. I think we're done.

01:04:39.819 --> 01:04:47.950
QUESTION: No, no. So—but if I read some
of the import of it, it seems like we're—we're

01:04:47.950 --> 01:04:54.230
bisecting ourselves between those geeks who
will create amazing things and those who are

01:04:54.230 --> 01:04:59.910
not able to enter that promised land, don't
have the drive, the intellectual capacity,

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:04.900
the interest, whatever limitation there is.
And yet, all those people will need something

01:05:04.900 --> 01:05:09.200
to do.
And I wonder, and I heard something that was

01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:14.180
interesting, which is, well, isn't part of
the potential of the machines to dumb down

01:05:14.180 --> 01:05:19.220
a whole bunch of tasks so that people can
feel like they're participating in this world

01:05:19.220 --> 01:05:24.260
and doing something useful, when in fact,
the machine is doing most of the work?

01:05:24.260 --> 01:05:29.940
I once read a story like that where a doctor
had this fantastic mechanized doctor's bag

01:05:29.940 --> 01:05:34.900
that was programmed with all medical knowledge
and he really believed he was a doctor, when,

01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:38.760
in fact, the doctor bag was the doctor. So
what do you think about that?

01:05:38.760 --> 01:05:44.710
BROOKS: I want to answer both questions at
once. One of the possibilities that will happen

01:05:44.710 --> 01:05:49.440
is that we will see the maker movement in
the same way we saw the Homebrew Computer

01:05:49.440 --> 01:05:55.640
Club takeover computation from the big companies,
the NCRs, the Honeywells, all those companies

01:05:55.640 --> 01:06:00.450
which were great computer companies, Deck,
et cetera. And that Homebrew Computer Club

01:06:00.450 --> 01:06:08.300
turned in to be the fountain of productivity.
The maker movement might turn into the fountain

01:06:08.300 --> 01:06:13.440
of productivity and there'll be much more—many
more people doing making than there have been

01:06:13.440 --> 01:06:18.770
in the past and a whole locus of where / what
makes a company may change, in the same way

01:06:18.770 --> 01:06:24.170
computer companies completely got obliterated
in the '80s by a whole new generation.

01:06:24.170 --> 01:06:32.359
And I'm not saying, you know, using a cell
phone analogy, making it easy to use doesn't

01:06:32.359 --> 01:06:38.430
mean it's dumbing it down so that people aren't
actually doing stuff with it. It just makes

01:06:38.430 --> 01:06:43.020
it more convenient to do things and you can
get up to the threshold of using them. I think

01:06:43.020 --> 01:06:47.890
that's different from fooling them. I'm not
arguing—I don't think it's they're fooling

01:06:47.890 --> 01:06:52.759
them. I think we can still provide useful
interfaces for people so they can become much

01:06:52.759 --> 01:06:58.099
more powerful and much more powerful in their
making. The same way if you're at Xerox, you

01:06:58.099 --> 01:07:02.670
know, the laser writer, the laser printer
let people do typesetting where they didn't—they

01:07:02.670 --> 01:07:08.740
couldn't do it before.
MCAFEE: What's interesting is that as the

01:07:08.740 --> 01:07:16.099
technologies have proliferated and really
democratized access to innovation and to making

01:07:16.099 --> 01:07:20.000
things—we've been doing this for thirty-plus
years—the economy has moved in the other

01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:24.320
direction. The economy has moved toward more
concentration, the big guys getting bigger,

01:07:24.320 --> 01:07:28.299
the workforce becoming more polarized instead
of less.

01:07:28.299 --> 01:07:32.119
So when I hear about this promised future
land of the maker movement that's going to

01:07:32.119 --> 01:07:36.200
restore middle class opportunity, it's arguing
exactly against the trends that are pretty

01:07:36.200 --> 01:07:41.690
clear in the evidence so far. So I'm really
worried about this polarization of opportunity

01:07:41.690 --> 01:07:46.559
and of mobility. I think it's already a challenge.
I think it's going to get a lot bigger. And

01:07:46.559 --> 01:07:51.819
what we do about that is going to be the challenge,
I believe, that defines us for the next generation

01:07:51.819 --> 01:07:54.930
or so.
The good news is the problem we're going to

01:07:54.930 --> 01:08:00.720
face is not the problem of scarcity, is not
the problem of not enough to go around. And

01:08:00.720 --> 01:08:05.559
I find it really important not to lose sight
of that fundamentally fantastic news. Keynes

01:08:05.559 --> 01:08:11.079
was right; we're going to get away with what
he called the economic problem of allocating

01:08:11.079 --> 01:08:16.130
in the face of scarcity. Fantastic. The distribution
problem is a tough problem, but like you know,

01:08:16.130 --> 01:08:20.320
we put a quote from Voltaire to start off
one of our chapters. He said that work saves

01:08:20.320 --> 01:08:27.100
us from three great evils; boredom, vice and
need. Of those three, I find the need easiest

01:08:27.100 --> 01:08:31.020
to take care of.
THOMPSON: Back there on the right?

01:08:31.020 --> 01:08:37.890
QUESTION: Thanks. Jamie Metzl, I'm an investor
and novelist. I would love if you could say

01:08:37.890 --> 01:08:49.080
a few words about the future of robotic pets.
BROOKS: Well, I do apologize for having let

01:08:49.080 --> 01:08:57.130
loose the genre of pets riding on Roombas
by having the Roomba, and therefore—people

01:08:57.130 --> 01:09:05.230
do treat their Roombas with a reverence that
I could not have believed. Hardcore—hardcore

01:09:05.230 --> 01:09:12.790
Marines in war zones treating their PackBot
with a reverence that I could not believe.

01:09:12.790 --> 01:09:23.910
So it is not too hard to trigger some of our
empathy for machines. And there will be startups

01:09:23.910 --> 01:09:27.300
which try to do that because they're going
to make a buck and so there will be machines

01:09:27.300 --> 01:09:34.600
around that some people treat as pets. Other
people have disdain for. We saw that just

01:09:34.600 --> 01:09:39.950
last week when Boston Dynamics, now owned
by Google, showed their new four-legged robot

01:09:39.950 --> 01:09:44.750
walking and the people were kicking it and
they got eviscerated in the press for --

01:09:44.750 --> 01:09:48.770
MCAFEE: For kicking the poor robot.
THOMPSON: All right. We have two minutes left.

01:09:48.770 --> 01:09:54.120
Last question here on the right, please?
QUESTION: Eyrique Miller from JPMorgan. I

01:09:54.120 --> 01:09:58.410
guess this ties into the last two questions.
So I have two daughters that are&nbsp;ten and

01:09:58.410 --> 01:10:03.770
five. They have a Zoomer, both of them, which
is the dog that you can teach and do tricks.

01:10:03.770 --> 01:10:07.170
And I guess my question is, and this is sort
of skipping the current generation of college

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:11.910
students, but are we doing enough for the
youth that are, you know, at the younger age

01:10:11.910 --> 01:10:16.310
to educate them in terms of robotics and actually
to be participants in where you think this

01:10:16.310 --> 01:10:19.630
is going?
MCAFEE: No, absolutely not. We're teaching

01:10:19.630 --> 01:10:21.430
--
QUESTION: But what can we be doing then if

01:10:21.430 --> 01:10:24.060
we're not doing enough?
GUPTA: We are not doing enough but there is,

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:27.760
I think, again—I agree that we are not doing
enough, first of all, &nbsp;I should say that.

01:10:27.760 --> 01:10:32.880
But I—for example, in Carnegie Mellon, I
see every day these capable students coming,

01:10:32.880 --> 01:10:38.100
like, four-year kids coming and making robots
in the labs. I think that is the best hope

01:10:38.100 --> 01:10:41.040
we have.
We need to, first of all—I mean, if you

01:10:41.040 --> 01:10:45.210
don't have to wait until college education
for robots to be inside their—inside your

01:10:45.210 --> 01:10:49.520
education. I think we need to take it much
faster. It could be—start with a very small

01:10:49.520 --> 01:10:54.350
system—small, like easy things they make
on. But I think there is—I mean, there should

01:10:54.350 --> 01:10:59.290
be something which could be done for that
this education goes to—starts as early as

01:10:59.290 --> 01:11:03.350
possible, rather than waiting until the last
moment, like the last four years, the bachelor

01:11:03.350 --> 01:11:08.800
technology or bachelors, to do this thing.
BROOKS: There is a little—and this is a—this

01:11:08.800 --> 01:11:14.040
is—you spoke as a parent. I'm going to speak
as a parent. One of my daughters has started

01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:19.230
a company to build STEM toys for girls age
seven to nine and it was actually at New York

01:11:19.230 --> 01:11:25.000
Toy Fair two weeks ago. There were a whole
bunch of actually women-run companies building

01:11:25.000 --> 01:11:30.500
engineering toys for girls. So we're starting
to see that. That may help the next generation

01:11:30.500 --> 01:11:34.450
be exposed as they're getting out. And they're
getting pretty wide distribution. So there

01:11:34.450 --> 01:11:40.710
are entrepreneurs wanting to do this. Her
major investor is Mark Cuban, but that's because

01:11:40.710 --> 01:11:46.680
he has daughters, too. So the UVCs—if you'll
just invest in girls' toys companies that

01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:48.100
will be a lot better.
THOMPSON: Elon Musk will not be investing

01:11:48.100 --> 01:11:50.830
after this panel.
Thank you very much. We all have to get back

01:11:50.830 --> 01:11:53.510
to our jobs before they're taken by robots.
Thank you to Malcolm and Carolyn Wiener and

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:54.410
thank you to our panel.
END

